Aim :- 

File Permission and Ownership: Understand and modify file permission.

Theory :- 

Linux uses file permission and ownership to control who can read, write or execute files. The ls -l, chmod, chown and chgrp commands are used to view and modify these permissions and ownership details.

Requirements:

•Linux Operating system
•Linux terminal
•A file for applying permissions and ownership
•Basic knowledge of Linux permission commands.

Command explanation :- 

a) View file permission using ls -l

$ ls -l filename.txt : Displays the file permissions, owner, group, size and other details.

b) Change permission using chmod (symbolic method)

$ chmod u+x filename.txt : Adds execute permission for the file owner using the symbolic method.

c) Change permission using chmod (numeric method)

$ chmod 785 filename.txt : Sets file permissions using numeric values for the owner, group and others.

d) Change ownership using chown and chgrp

$ sudo chown username filename.txt : Changes the owner of the specified file.

$ sudo chgrp groupname filename.txt : Changes the group ownership of the specified file.

